ID produces a reminder — a batch that touches the same Story's status twice
(for example a reopen side effect followed by the requested transition) still
emits exactly one reminder for it. Distinct qualifying Story IDs each get
their own reminder, in first-seen order.

### Payload shape

```json
{
  "code": "story_worktree_base_preflight_required",
  "timestamp": "2026-08-29T12:34:56.123456789Z",
  "message": "Resolve and refresh the configured integration branch upstream, compare exact OIDs, and record the selected Story worktree base before provisioning or trusting it.",
  "story_id": "STORY-260218-bbbbbb",
  "trunk_ref": "refs/heads/main",
  "trunk_oid": "8c3f1a9...",
  "upstream_ref": "refs/remotes/protected/main",
  "upstream_oid": "5e2b7d0...",
  "selected_base_oid": null
}
```

`code`, `message`, and the RFC3339Nano UTC `timestamp` are stable across every
reminder in one mutation (one clock read per mutation, not per Story).
`trunk_ref`, `trunk_oid`, `upstream_ref`, `upstream_oid`, and
`selected_base_oid` are always present as JSON keys and are explicit `null`,
never omitted, whenever that specific piece of evidence is not known. The
`agent_reminders` key itself is only added to the result when at least one
change qualified; a mutation with no qualifying activation adds no key at all
(not an empty array).

### Authority evidence: local versus remote

The five nullable fields come from a cache-only projection that is read after
the mutation persists and never fails the mutation on error:

- **Local CLI** — prefers a persisted workspace freshness record from a prior
  `task-board spawn` for that Story (`trunk_ref`/`trunk_oid` = the recorded
  integration branch and its local OID at spawn time, `upstream_ref`/
  `upstream_oid` = the recorded upstream ref/OID, `selected_base_oid` = the
  OID the workspace actually branched from). When no such record exists, it
  falls back to a cache-only Git discovery (`DiscoverCachedTrunk`) against the
  configured `integration_base_branch` — the same discovery order as the
  freshness gate, but it never fetches, so `upstream_oid` reflects whatever
  the last fetch already cached — and `selected_base_oid` is `null`, because
  a cache-only projection names no provisioned workspace. Any read failure
  (unreadable tracking config, unreadable ref) renders every field `null`
  rather than failing the mutation.
- **Remote (board-server)** — the reminder is produced by the identical shared
  `pkg/board/query` mutation code, so it fires on the same triggers with the
  same dedup, but the server wires no authority source (there is no local Git
  checkout to read), so every one of the five fields is always `null`; only
  `code`, `timestamp`, `message`, and `story_id` are populated.

### Mutation coverage

`agent_reminders` is decorated by one shared boundary that a subset of
production mutations route their persisted changes through:

| Covered — can emit a reminder | Not wired — structurally cannot emit |
| --- | --- |
| `set_status` | `normalize_legacy_status` |
| `handoff` | `set_estimate` |
| `create` (only via its ancestor-reopen side effects) | `set_details`, `rename` |
| `delete` | `check_item`, `uncheck_item`, `add_checklist_item` |
| `set_parent` | `set_notes` |
| `assign`, `unassign`, `link`, `unlink` (wired, but their own changes are never `status` changes, so they only ever forward an ancestor side effect) | context mutations |

`assign`/`unassign`/`link`/`unlink` are listed on the covered side because
they share the same decorator plumbing as `set_status`; they simply never
produce a qualifying change themselves. The "not wired" column takes no `cfg`
at all in `registerMutations`, so it cannot mint a reminder even if a caller
crafted a matching change shape.

## Landing a Story on trunk

This section extends the commit-confirmation contract above to the one path
where a `done` transition is produced by a command rather than typed by hand:
`task-board worktree integrate`, which lands a Story developed in a managed
Story workspace. The mechanics of the managed workspace itself live in
[tracked background spawn](tracked-background-spawn.md#story-worktree-isolation);
what belongs here is the ordering and the exact scope of the acknowledgement.

**Accepted comes before done, always.** A reviewer records acceptance with
`accept_cr`, which parks the element at `to-review` as the accepted handoff. No
Git operation has happened at that point and nothing is `done`. The
Orchestrator then integrates, and the `done` transition is the *last* board
effect of a run that has already moved trunk. The superseded order — close the
board first, land the work afterwards — is withdrawn, because it produced an
acknowledgement that attested a scope no repository had yet seen.

```bash
task-board worktree integrate STORY-260205-def456 \
  --cr TASK-260205-abc123 --revision 3 \
  --commit-time 2026-08-06T20:01:00+03:00
```

**The landing gate defaults to refusing.** `integrate` requires the
`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` suite to be configured; with no
suite it refuses `validation_not_configured` before any ref moves. A project
with nothing to run states that explicitly as `{"commands": []}`. See
[task-board config](task-board-config.md#the-landing-gate-suite).

**Squash, fast-forward-only, and one explicit time.** The accepted Story
candidate lands as one squash commit on trunk; the branch's internal Task
checkpoints are discarded rather than replayed, and trunk is only ever
fast-forwarded. When `version_control.confirm` is `true`, `--commit-time` is
required, must parse as RFC3339, and is applied to the author date and the
committer date of both commits this command creates. The example above is this
repository's own policy expressed concretely: the previous day at 20:01 MSK,
that is `2026-08-06T20:01:00+03:00` for an integration run on 2026-08-07. With
confirmation disabled and no flag, the current time is used instead.

**What the acknowledgement asserts here, and what it does not.** The command
supplies `commit_ack=scope_committed` on the `done` transition itself, and that
transition is not reachable until the Story's squash commit is genuinely
contained in trunk — a resumed run whose trunk no longer carries the recorded
candidate refuses rather than stamping the acknowledgement. So the assertion is
exactly: *the repository scope of this Story is on trunk.* It is narrower than
a clean tree in three specific ways, and each of them is a normal, expected
state at the moment the acknowledgement is written:

- **The Story's own board delta is still uncommitted.** The board-state commit
  is created *after* the transition, precisely because the transition is what
  produces the board bytes it records.
- **Board paths outside this Story are untouched.** The second commit stages an
  explicit pathspec: this Story's own subtree, the resource directories of its
  elements, and the ancestor aggregation files on its chain. Everything else
  under `.task-board/` — `.task-board/.goals/**` and every other Story's
  subtree — stays exactly as it was: ordinary uncommitted working-tree state
  that this command neither examines nor stages.
- **Foreign uncommitted work is untouched**, on the same rule the section above
  states for a hand-typed acknowledgement.

**The two commits, in order.** First the squash commit carrying the accepted
candidate tree; then the `done` transition; then a second, board-only commit
titled `Record <STORY-ID> board state`. A Story whose accepted candidate has no
repository delta produces no first commit and still produces the second one —
its board record is the whole of its work product.

**Recovery is re-running the same command.** Integration keeps one durable
transaction record per Story, so a crashed or interrupted run resumes the
recorded phase instead of starting a second integration, and a completed one is
a no-op rather than a duplicate landing. A rollback is offered in exactly one
phase — nothing landed yet, trunk unmoved — and is requested explicitly with
`--rollback`; no later phase undoes a commit that is already on trunk. Partial
integration is therefore a state to resume, never a state to clean up by hand.
# Resources

Resources are owned by the board element they are attached to. Use explicit
CRUD operations against that element ID. Local payloads are grouped under
`.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/`; remote board-server uses the equivalent
element-scoped filesystem or MinIO object-key prefix.

## Resource Types

| Type | Purpose | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| **precondition** | Inputs needed before work starts | Design docs, specs, instructions, API schemas |
| **outcome** | Results produced by work | Research findings, implementation notes, analysis |

## Commands

```bash
# Create a resource
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_design.md, type=precondition, content="...", description="Architecture design")'
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content="...", description="Research findings")'

# Replace an existing resource
task-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content="revised findings", description="Research findings v2")'
task-board resource update TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_results.md --name reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome

# Adopt an existing orphaned local payload without rewriting it
task-board resource adopt TASK-260218-abc revisions/2/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome --description "Recovered result"

# Delete a resource (destructive — requires --confirm)
task-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md)'
task-board resource delete TASK-260218-abc artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md --confirm

# View resources on an element
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { preconditionResources outcomeResources }'
```

## Element-Owned Storage

Resources from different tasks are not stored in one shared file pile. Each
element gets its own directory, and every resource attached to that element is
stored below it:

```
.task-board/.resources/
├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/
│   ├── findings/
│   │   └── investigation.md
│   ├── artifacts/
│   │   └── run-1/
│   │       └── output.json
│   ├── reports/
│   │   └── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md
│   └── revisions/
│       └── 2/
│           └── review.md
└── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/
    └── research.md         # outcome
```

The resource `name` is a safe forward-slash-relative path. Agents may choose
the hierarchy that fits the artifact and nest it further as needed. Every
segment must be non-empty printable UTF-8 and must not be `.`, `..`, an
absolute/drive-qualified path, or contain a backslash. Raw forward slashes are
the only hierarchy delimiter; malformed URL escapes and encoded or repeatedly
encoded separators/traversal are rejected. Attach related files to the same
owning element; do not invent a generic board-wide resource task or write
payloads into `.task-board/.resources/` manually.

Directories are structural nodes, not payloads. Resource CRUD, validation, and
handoff evidence recognize regular files only; a directory named like a report
cannot be adopted or used to satisfy outcome evidence.

Local and board-server filesystem element deletion is journaled and
crash-recoverable. Direct resource CRUD first resolves any pending staged
element delete under the board mutation lock. MinIO element/subtree deletion
currently fails before mutation with the typed
`ErrAtomicResourceDeleteUnsupported` capability error; ordinary resource CRUD
remains available.

References are tracked in `progress.md` as markdown links:
```markdown
## Precondition Resources
- [specs/auth/design.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/precondition/specs/auth/design.md) — Architecture design

## Outcome Resources
- [reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/outcome/reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md) — Research findings
```

## Spawn Integration

Precondition resources are automatically injected into agent prompts by `task-board spawn`. Full prompts preserve normal text inlining. Lite prompts use the resolved `agent_context.prompt.preconditions` policy: by default, text below `inline_bytes` is rendered inline, while larger text and binary resources are written read-only under `.temp/resources/` and represented by their name, description, byte count, SHA-256 digest, and local path. This means:

1. Attach instructions/specs as precondition resources to a task
2. Run `task-board spawn TASK-XX --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` — agent gets everything automatically
3. Agent saves results via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write, then `update_resource(...)` for revisions

## Best Practices

- **Use preconditions for agent instructions** — attach design docs, specs, or step-by-step instructions before spawning
- **Use outcomes for work products** — research findings, analysis, implementation notes
- **Attach to the owning element** — pass the Task, Bug, Story, or Epic ID that owns the artifact; the storage layer groups its payloads under that element directory automatically
- **Use explicit create/update/delete** — `add_resource` / `resource add` must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` when replacing an existing payload
- **Use adopt only for local repair** — `resource adopt` registers bytes already stored at `.resources/<elementID>/<relative-resource-path>` without rewriting them; a missing payload returns resource-not-found and an existing declaration returns resource-exists
- **Choose a useful hierarchy inside the owning element** — `findings/`, `artifacts/`, `reports/`, and `revisions/` are conventions, not a fixed schema; nest further when it makes the handoff easier to navigate
- **Use safe relative resource paths** — `/` separates validated path segments; empty, dot/traversal, absolute/drive-qualified, backslash, control-character, malformed escape, and encoded/repeatedly encoded separator or traversal forms are rejected in local and remote storage
- **Attach files, not folders** — directories only organize resource files and never satisfy resource existence or handoff evidence
- **Always use board commands** — never create/edit resource files or progress.md resource sections manually
- **Name handoff evidence with task scope** — keep the task ID in the resource path, for example `reports/TASK-260218-abc_architecture-design.md`, so `task-board handoff` can recognize it
- **Add descriptions** — the `description=` param adds a one-line description shown in spawn prompts

---

# Bugs

Bugs are elements of the board, living inside stories alongside tasks.

```bash
# Create bug
task-board m 'create(type=bug, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=crash-on-start, description="App crashes when starting recording")'

# Track like any element
task-board m 'set_status(BUG-260218-abc, status=development)'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Reproduce the issue")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Find root cause")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Write fix")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Add regression test")'
```
# Negative Evidence Contract

A passing suite means nothing unless something in it would have failed. This
file is the single source of truth for the rules that make a green check mean
something, and for the incident record behind each one. The operative wording
that reaches every spawned child lives in the spawn template section
`Evidence That Counts`; the reviewer's licence lives in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`;
the repeated-variant heuristic lives in `.roles/orchestrator/role.md`. Read this
file when you need the shapes, the failure vocabulary, or the incidents that
produced a rule — do not restate these rules in other documents.

| Rule | Operative statement | Evidence record |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Negative tests for gates | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Narrowing, not deleting | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Production call site | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Absence vs failure to read | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Prove, or report nothing | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Reviewer licence to defeat a gate | `.roles/reviewer/role.md` | this file |
| Many variants, one unowned decision | `.roles/orchestrator/role.md` | this file |

## Negative Tests For Gates

Any feature that GATES, REFUSES, VALIDATES, AUTHORIZES, or ATTESTS must ship
with negative tests that fail when the gate admits what it is supposed to
reject. A positive test proving the happy path passes is not evidence that the
gate works; it is evidence that the gate is reachable.

This is the dominant defect class in this repository's 2026-07-28..08-03 waves.
Every instance below was found because a reviewer deliberately attacked the
gate, not because anyone read the code:

- the fact-assay attested a COLD child that never inherited the context, because
  it checked inventory, session id, digests, and output shape, but never
  inheritance;
- the assay fork had repository tool access, so it could answer from disk while
  the base was false;
- the builder attestation accepted a correctly-shaped forged digest that the
  caller minted itself;
- clearance enforcement existed but no production path called it, and counting
  tests around a fake proved nothing;
- an unknown assay event preserved a false zero-tool observation;
- a provider capability was declared absent to disable a flaky check, and the
  claim did not reproduce (9/11 success).

## Standard Negative Shapes

Use these names in checklists, verdicts, and test names. They are the shapes a
negative test must cover and the shapes a reviewer looks for first.

| Shape | What it looks like when nobody tests for it | What the negative test must do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Forged or self-minted evidence | The caller supplies the proof that the gate checks, and a correctly-shaped value passes. | Mint the evidence in the test the way a caller could, and require refusal. |
| Absent evidence treated as satisfied | Missing input takes the permissive branch; the suite only ever supplies the input. | Remove the evidence entirely and require refusal, not a default-pass. |
| Check present but uncalled from production | The guard is implemented and unit-tested by direct call; no production path invokes it. | Drive the real entry point — launch, materialize, resolve, publish — and name the production call site. |
| Bypass path around the check | One entry point enforces; a second, older, or retry/renewal path does not. | Exercise every entry point that reaches the protected state, including retry, renewal, resume, and recovery, and every delivery surface that carries the rule — a contract clause reaching one context profile and not the other is this shape. |
| Capability claim that does not reproduce | A capability is declared absent or present to make a flaky check go away. | Re-run the claim enough times to state a real rate, or report unknown. |

## Prove A Bound By Narrowing, Not By Deleting

A test that removes a gate entirely proves only that the gate exists. It says
nothing about whether the gate covers the right class. Restrict the gate to a
narrower condition instead and require a named test to fail.

Two repository incidents where a narrowed gate survived a green suite:

- **BUG-260801-3kiloe F1** — the recovery successor cap was proven only by total
  removal. Narrowing it to provider-limit signals left every other recoverable
  failure unbounded, with the whole suite still green.
- **TASK-260802-17wetr F16** — thirteen cycles later, the same shape: the
  probe-lease cap survived deletion tests while the renewal path escaped it.
- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 1** — this contract itself. The reviewer clauses
  landed in the role body and the shipped test checked the role file and the
  lite template separately, so both halves were green while the composed lite
  prompt carried neither. Assert on the composed production artifact, not on the
  pieces it is built from.
- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 2** — the same contract, one level up, and the
  clearest statement of the difference between the two shapes. The cycle-1 fix
  did assert on the composed prompt, but it called the composition helper
  directly and handed it the role, task-class filter, and profile itself.
  Production builds those inputs inside the spawn entry point, so a mutant
  narrowing only the real lite-reviewer routing just before composition left the
  test green while an actual lite reviewer lost its gate-defeat licence. A test
  that supplies the inputs is testing the helper, not the path. Obtain the
  artifact the way the consumer does — here, read the prompt file recorded on
  the run manifest after driving the entry point — so role resolution, the
  task-class filter, and profile resolution are all under test rather than
  assumed.

Three traps when running the mutants themselves, all hit while proving this file:

- **The mutant must be re-run with `-count=1`.** `go test` caches on package
  sources; a test that reads `.roles/*.md` or any file outside its package at
  runtime returns a stale cached PASS after that file is mutated. A cached green
  is not a surviving mutant, it is an unexecuted test.
- **A passing assertion may be satisfied by the wrong source.** Two mutants of
  the producer DoD entries survived because the phrase asserted also appears,
  differently worded, in the shared spawn template. Choose assertion wording
  that only the intended source can supply, then narrow that source and require
  the named test to fail.
- **Restore the mutant from a copy, not from git.** `git checkout <file>`
  restores from the index, which silently discards uncommitted work in that file
  — during this task it reverted the whole cycle-2 change, not just the mutant.
  Copy the file aside before mutating, restore from the copy, and verify the
  restoration by diffing against it before rerunning the gates.

## An Absence And A Failure To Read Are Different Facts

Absence is a legitimate state with a defined fallback. A failed, partial, or
malformed read is evidence of a problem. Treating the second as the first lets
the fallback launder a fault into a normal-looking value, and a fallback defined
for absence must never fire on a read failure.

Each of these was a separate defect in this repository:

- a non-empty value read as verified;
- an unknown scanner version read as clean;
- a partial provenance record read as absent;
- a zero pid start time read as alive;
- a malformed `api_error_status` read as missing;
- an unparsed envelope read as no envelope — this one produced a false provider
  suppression that would have hidden a healthy provider for hours.

## Prove, Or Report Nothing

Where a property cannot be established, report unparsed or unknown rather than
inferring it from a proxy signal. A plausible guess is worse than an explicit
unknown, because callers act on it.

Incident: headless top-level inference in the Claude classifier was unsound in
principle and produced six variants before it was deleted rather than
strengthened.

## Many Variants, One Unowned Decision

A long run of same-shaped variants usually means a decision has more than one
owner, not that there are many bugs. Eleven framing variants stopped reproducing
only when structure got a single scanner and a single state type; every per-site
patch before that produced the next variant. The operative wording is in
`.roles/orchestrator/role.md`: unify the decision, do not patch each site.

## Reviewer Licence

Reviewers are explicitly licensed to attempt to defeat a gate and to reject a
change whose only evidence is positive-path. Grep for callers before believing a
guard is in force. The operative wording is in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`.
# Role Registry

Roles define a spawned agent's identity, skills, lifecycle, and baseline
definition of done. The registry supports project-local overrides and bundled
global defaults without copying role bodies into `SKILL.md`.

## Registry Layout

The preferred directory form keeps the role definition and optional skill
catalog together:

```text
.roles/
├── developer/
│   ├── role.md
│   └── skills.md
├── researcher/
│   ├── role.md
│   └── skills.md
└── reviewer/
    └── role.md
```

The legacy single-file form, `.roles/<role-name>.md`, remains supported.

## `role.md` Frontmatter

```yaml
---
name: developer
description: Writes code, fixes bugs, writes tests
archetype: implementer
start_status: development
end_status: to-review
dod:
  - Outcome attached with a task-scoped name
  - text: Relevant tests pass
    classes: [code]
  - text: Published artifact validated
    classes: [metadata, docs]
skills:
  - project-management
---

# developer

The Markdown body supplies the role's instructions, deliverable, standing
orders, constraints, and human-facing handoff wording.
```

Keep baseline DoD only in frontmatter; spawn merges it with the task checklist
and renders it once. Do not repeat a `Definition of Done` section in the body.
For board reads, role bodies direct concrete workers to compact task-specific
projections and away from routine `summary()`, `plan()`, `schema()`, and
`{ full }` orientation.

Frontmatter fields:

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `name` | Canonical role identity used by `--role`. |
| `description` | Short role summary used in role listings. |
| `archetype` | High-level identity: `implementer`, `analyst`, `reviewer`, or `tester`. |
| `start_status` | FIRST lifecycle status set after the child launches. Omit for an orchestration-only role. |
| `end_status` | LAST producer handoff status. A verdict-driven reviewer omits it and selects exactly one verdict branch instead. |
| `dod` | Baseline checklist entries injected by spawn, optionally gated by task class. |
| `skills` | Optional inline lazy skill catalog, merged with `skills.md`. |

## Task Classes And DoD Gating

Every board element has a `task_class`:

- `code` — default, including legacy elements without an explicit value
- `metadata`
- `docs`
- `research`

Set it during creation or update it later:

```bash
task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc123, name=publish-metadata, task_class=metadata)'
task-board m 'set_details(TASK-260218-def456, task_class=docs)'
```

Inspect it through the `task_class` query projection:

```bash
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-def456) { id name task_class }'
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task) { id name task_class }'
```

A role `dod` entry may be either:

- a plain string, which applies to every task class; or
- an object with `text` and optional `classes`.

Spawn injects an object entry only when the board element's normalized
`task_class` appears in `classes`. Omitting `classes` or using an empty list
preserves the all-classes behavior. Unknown class values are rejected. This
filter applies to role baseline DoD; the task-specific checklist already stored
on the board remains the task-specific contract.

```yaml
dod:
  - Always attach a task-scoped outcome
  - text: Run code tests
    classes: [code]
  - text: Validate the published artifact
    classes:
      - metadata
      - docs
```

## Negative Evidence In Role DoD

Baseline DoD is where a cross-cutting evidence rule becomes a producer default
instead of something an orchestrator repeats in every prompt. The negative
testing rule is wired this way:

- `developer` and `tester` carry a `code`-gated `dod` entry requiring negative
  tests for gating, refusing, validating, authorizing, and attesting behavior.
- `reviewer` carries a `dod` entry refusing positive-path-only evidence, and its
  body grants the licence to attempt to defeat a gate.
- The operative rules reach every spawned child once, through the spawn
  template's `Evidence That Counts` section; the lite context profile carries the
  compressed form.
- The shapes vocabulary and the incident record behind each rule live once in
  [negative evidence](negative-evidence.md).

Reference that file from a role rather than copying its rules into a role body.

## `skills.md` Format

`skills.md` is optional. Use the `Required` and `Domain` sections so agents can
distinguish baseline workflow skills from task-dependent domain catalogs:

```markdown
# developer — skills

## Required

- project-management
- go-testing-tools

## Domain

- core-data (when working on persistence)
- swiftui (when building UI)
```

List entries are merged with the frontmatter `skills` list and deduplicated.
Skills remain lazy references: load a full skill body only when the concrete
task scope requires it or the user, task, or project explicitly names it.

## Resolution Order

`LoadRole(workDir, name)` resolves the first existing path in this exact order:

1. Project directory: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>/role.md`
2. Project single file: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>.md`
3. Global directory: `~/.roles/<name>/role.md`
4. Global single file: `~/.roles/<name>.md`

Therefore project-local definitions override global definitions, and the
directory form overrides the single-file form within the same registry. Role
names are normalized before lookup; path-like or unknown roles fail closed.
Only the directory form loads a sibling `skills.md`.

## Bundled Roles And Global Installation

The bundled registry includes these core roles:

| Role | Archetype | Lifecycle |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `researcher` | analyst | `analysis` -> `to-review` |
| `developer` | implementer | `development` -> `to-review` |
| `tester` | tester | `development` -> `to-review` |
| `solution-architect` | analyst | `analysis` -> `to-review` |
| `doc-writer` | implementer | `development` -> `to-review` |
| `reviewer` | reviewer | `reviewing` -> verdict branch |
| `orchestrator` | analyst | orchestration-only |
| `coordinator` | analyst | legacy alias for `orchestrator` |

Run the canonical setup entrypoint from the skill source checkout to sync
bundled `.roles/` entries into `~/.roles/`:

```bash
./scripts/setup.sh --install-only
```

Setup installs the skill artifact and globally available roles, then verifies
the installed role trees and the complete installed `references/` directory
against source. Because reference parity is recursive, it includes this file.

